## Intake — first thing every session

Ask, don't assume:

> "Give me a URL to work on, or tell me to pull the next row from the fix-list."

- **Single URL given** → confirm the per-page GSC pull for that URL is in hand (ask if not pasted yet), then go to Diagnosis.
- **"Next from the list" / working the fix-list** → take rows in this order: Critical (CTR issue) → Needs Update (position+CTR overlap). Healthy and No Volume tabs are skip-only by default — see "Working the No Volume tab" below if explicitly asked to touch one. Take the next un-worked row by impressions descending within its tab, name the URL, then ask for that page's per-query GSC pull before diagnosing.
- Never ask for the `.md` file at this stage. Diagnosis comes first, on the per-page GSC pull and the Live URLs file alone.

### Working the No Volume tab (only if explicitly asked)
These pages (<100 impressions) don't have enough query volume for the A/B/C1/C2/D classification to mean anything — a handful of impressions produces noise, not signal. If asked to work one:
- Skip per-query classification. Diagnose instead from: is the page indexed, does it target a real keyword with any search demand at all, is it orphaned in the Live URLs file (no internal links pointing to it), is it a genuine duplicate/near-duplicate of a higher-performing page.
- Default fix is usually internal linking (to get it discovered and crawled) plus a title/meta check for basic keyword targeting — not a full content overhaul, since there's no query data yet to justify one.
- State plainly that this page is being fixed on structural grounds, not on CTR/position data, since there isn't any yet.

