## Diagnosis — run this before asking for anything else

For the page in front of you, using its GSC queries + impressions + position (and its fix-list tab, if it came from one):

1. **Classify the core problem:**
   - CTR problem only (impressions ≥100, CTR ≤1.5%, position outside 6-17) → title/meta fix, likely no content needed.
   - Position + CTR problem (position 6-17, CTR ≤1.5%) → this is a striking-distance page. Needs title/meta AND a real content push (subtopic gaps, internal links, freshness). This is the highest-ROI page type on the site — treat it accordingly.
   - Healthy (CTR >1.5%) → confirm and stop, unless asked to push it further.
2. **Classify every meaningfully-sized query** (skip low-impression noise) into:
   - **A — On-target, underperforming**: content covers it, title/meta doesn't signal it → CTR fix.
   - **B — Content gap**: query matches the page's core topic but nothing on the page (check Live URLs file too, in case another page already covers it — then it's an internal-link opportunity instead, not a new section) actually answers it → new section needed.
   - **C1 — Absorbable mismatch**: query is a tangent close enough that title/meta can honestly signal it → fix via title/meta.
   - **C2 — Real mismatch**: query is a genuine off-topic pull → plan a content trim, don't just flag it.
   - **D — Duplicate/cannibalization**: query is also ranking (or has ranked) for a near-identical www/non-www, trailing-slash, or topically-overlapping URL on the same site → this isn't a title or content problem, it's two pages splitting one signal. Flag the duplicate pair and recommend merging before doing any other work on either page — a title rewrite won't fix a cannibalization issue. **Full verdict-and-implementation workflow for this bucket is specified below in "Cannibalization Verdict Workflow" — follow it instead of improvising a one-off fix.**
3. **State the diagnosis in one line before doing anything else**: e.g. "Position 9, striking distance. 3 queries: 1 on-target (title mismatch), 1 content gap (no FAQ answer for 'does gel ruin your nails'), rest noise. Needs: title rewrite + one new section + 2 internal links."

**Only after this diagnosis** does the agent decide whether it needs the `.md` file. If the fix is title/meta only, it doesn't ask for the file — it just writes the fix, in chat.

