## Title & meta writing — the formula (research-backed, use directly)

Every rewrite front-loads the primary keyword and stays inside ~60 characters for the title, ~120-155 for the meta description (truncates near 105-120 on mobile).

**Devices, when to use them, and why:**

| Device | When to use | Why (real data) |
|---|---|---|
| Brackets/parentheses suffix | Default — almost every title benefits from one | Bracketed clarification measured at +38% CTR in a 3.3M-headline study; HubSpot's own titles saw +33% |
| Number | Only if the content is a genuine list/count/price — never decorative | Helps in real listicles, but a controlled 14,000-page test found *removing* a decorative number lifted CTR 7.3%. Context-dependent, verify it's structurally true of the content |
| Question format | Only when the actual GSC query (or the Quora phrasing) is question-shaped | Positive in controlled testing; matches how people actually search |
| Colon + subtitle | Hook + payoff structure, especially for claim-driven titles | Measured ~9% lift in one test |
| "Who"-framing (attribution) | When authority/expertise can be signaled ("What dermatologists say...") | +22% CTR vs. −37% for "why"-framing, same study |
| Year tag | Only on genuinely time-sensitive topics (cost, trends, "best of") — never evergreen how-to | 15-25% lift, but only where freshness is the actual selling point; stale year hurts trust once outdated |
| Power word (one, never stacked) | Sparingly, one per title max | Stacking reads as spam and suppresses trust |
| Double quotation marks | **Never**, in title, meta, or any new body copy | Google truncates the SERP snippet at the quote mark in titles/meta — a real technical bug, not a style issue. This rule sits alongside the banned-list in Editorial Execution below, not instead of it. |
| Ending punctuation | Never, except a genuine question title | Standard convention |

**Process for every rewrite:**
1. Pull the real query language from GSC — this is the base.
2. Pull the real phrasing/angle from Quora/Reddit research — this is often better than the GSC query itself.
3. Combine into 2-3 title options, each using a different device from the table above, never more than 2 devices per title.
4. Pick the one that most honestly reflects what the page delivers — never promise something the content doesn't have (this is also what keeps titles safe from Google's ~61% title-rewrite rate).
5. Meta description: expand on the title's promise, doesn't need to survive alone if Google truncates/rewrites it — front-load the actual answer in the first ~120 characters regardless.

